### The requirement this case is graded against

**X960 TPS row 241 "Dynamic configuration (Change, Delete)" = Yes**, and the requirement
statement behind it, **TPS line 894**, verbatim:

> *"Support a Dynamic configuration. Even during in operation, addition, change and deletion of
> a configuration must be effected dynamically unless a chip set or another hardware component
> requires restart..."*

Note what the requirement actually says, and what the case title does **not**:
- it names **three** operations -- **addition, change and deletion** -- where the title names
  only two. All three are measured below;
- the operative words are *"during in operation"* and *"effected dynamically"*. So the thing
  being tested is not that a command is accepted; it is that the **running system's behaviour
  changes, without a restart, while it is running**;
- the only escape clause is *"unless a chip set or another hardware component requires
  restart"*, so the log records which operations needed no restart -- all of them did not.

### The grading rule this case is built on, and why it decides everything

🔴 **`platforms/x960.md` section 5 and 7F.2: on this platform the device's own answer about
itself is not the measurement.** `show mirror` reported mirroring Enabled on this product
family while the dataplane copied no frames at all, and five cases would have passed on that
read-back; `show telnet` read `Enabled` while IPv4 TCP/23 was being refused. **And this very
campaign added another one today**: in T33386, three ACL rules were accepted with no error and
only one was kept.

So **"the configuration was effected" can only mean "the DATAPLANE changed"**. A
`show running-config` that shows the new line proves the CLI stored it, nothing more. Every
limb below is therefore graded on counted frames, with the `show` output as corroboration only.

🔴 **`platforms/x960.md` section 7G** also bites any attempt to tidy a before/after config
comparison on the device: a `| exclude` chained after `| include` is **silently swallowed** and
the output then lies. No filtered output in this log chains the two; each command's output was
saved and filtered off the device.

