## The method: two independent subsystems, each taking all three operations

One subsystem could be a special case. Two unrelated ones, one in the **forwarding filter** and
one in the **layer-3 interface**, is evidence about the property the requirement actually
claims -- that configuration in general takes effect dynamically.

| | subsystem A -- a hardware forwarding filter | subsystem B -- a layer-3 interface address |
| --- | --- | --- |
| what is configured | `access-list hardware ckdyn` + `access-group` on `port1.0.10` | `ip address` on `interface vlan48` |
| measured on | `eth3`, TCP SYN to `10.38.22.34:22` and ICMP echo to `10.38.22.34` | `eth4`, ARP who-has and ICMP echo to `10.38.22.66` and `10.38.22.70` |
| the in-leg control | the two flows: in **every** state exactly one must pass, so a dead instrument cannot look like a pass | `eth3` to `vlan10`, which this subsystem never touches |
| instrument | `tools/arpleg.py` (its `--tcp` leg was added by T33386 earlier in this group) | `tools/arpleg.py` `--arp` / `--icmp` |

Every leg sends a **known count** (4 per flow) and counts what comes back, per
STANDING-ORDERS section 7. A `ping` could not do any of this: it cannot select the TCP port, it
cannot carry a non-matching control in the same run, and it gives no per-frame record.

## STEP 1 -- baseline, before any change: what each subsystem looks like untouched

```
sudo arpleg.py --tag dyn-base --out /tmp/mgmt1159/T47192 --iface eth3 \
     --tcp 10.38.22.34:22 --icmp 10.38.22.34 --sender-ip 10.38.22.33 \
     --sender-mac 00:0d:b9:55:77:0e --gw-mac e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 --count 4 --wait 4
  cap dyn-base-eth3.pcap: 22 frame(s)
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.33    :40000 -> 10.38.22.34    :22    S    [ours]
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.34    :22    -> 10.38.22.33    :40000 SA
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.33    :40000 -> 10.38.22.34    :22    R
    x4     ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.33     -> 10.38.22.34     [ours]
    x4     ICMP t0/c0 10.38.22.34     -> 10.38.22.33     [ours]
    x1     ARP-REQ  who-has 10.38.22.34     tell 10.38.22.33
    x1     ARP-REPLY 10.38.22.34     is-at e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43
```
**Subsystem A baseline: BOTH flows pass.** TCP/22 answered `SA` 4 of 4, ICMP answered 4 of 4.

```
sudo arpleg.py --tag dyn-base4 --out /tmp/mgmt1159/T47192 --iface eth4 \
     --arp 10.38.22.66,10.38.22.70 --icmp 10.38.22.66,10.38.22.70 \
     --sender-ip 10.38.22.65 --sender-mac 00:0d:b9:55:77:0f \
     --gw-mac e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 --count 4 --wait 4
  cap dyn-base4-eth4.pcap: 31 frame(s)
    x4     ARP-REQ  who-has 10.38.22.66     tell 10.38.22.65
    x4     ARP-REPLY 10.38.22.66     is-at e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43        <- .66 EXISTS
    x4     ARP-REQ  who-has 10.38.22.70     tell 10.38.22.65        <- .70 does not: no reply
    x4     ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.65     -> 10.38.22.66     [ours]
    x4     ICMP t0/c0 10.38.22.66     -> 10.38.22.65     [ours]     <- echo REPLY from .66
    x4     ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.65     -> 10.38.22.70     [ours]
    x4     ICMP t3/c1 10.38.22.66     -> 10.38.22.65     [ours]     <- host UNREACHABLE, from .66
    x3     ARP-REQ  who-has 10.38.22.70     tell 10.38.22.66
```
**Subsystem B baseline: `.66` exists, `.70` does not**, and the signature of each is distinct:
an existing address answers ARP and echoes; a non-existent one draws no ARP reply and makes the
DUT emit **ICMP type 3 code 1 (host unreachable) sourced from the address it does have**.

**STEP 1 verdict: PASS (baseline established on both subsystems).**

