```
awplus(config)#access-list hardware ckdyn
awplus(config-ip-hw-acl)#deny tcp any any eq 22
awplus(config-ip-hw-acl)#exit
awplus(config)#interface port1.0.10
awplus(config-if)#access-group ckdyn
awplus(config-if)#exit
awplus(config)#end                                   [box 14:16:15 NZDT]
```
No `% ` line; `cfg.py` exited 0.

```
sudo arpleg.py --tag dyn-add ... --iface eth3 --tcp 10.38.22.34:22 --icmp 10.38.22.34 ... --count 4
  cap dyn-add-eth3.pcap: 12 frame(s)
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.33    :40000 -> 10.38.22.34    :22    S    [ours]
    x4     ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.33     -> 10.38.22.34     [ours]
    x4     ICMP t0/c0 10.38.22.34     -> 10.38.22.33     [ours]
```

| flow | STEP 1 | after the ADDITION | reading |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| TCP/22, 4 SYNs | **4 `SA`** | **0 replies of any kind** | the new rule is **in the dataplane** |
| ICMP echo, 4 (the in-leg control) | 4 replies | **4 replies** | the port and the capture are alive, the filter is selective -- the silence is a measurement, not a dead instrument |

**The leg's first frame left the testbox a few seconds after the `end`**, and the rule was
already enforced. No reload, no `write`, no restart of any kind.

**STEP 2 verdict: PASS -- addition is effected dynamically.**

## STEP 3 -- CHANGE: the bound filter is edited in place and the two flows SWAP state

This is the strongest form of the test: not "remove then re-add", but a **change to a filter
that is currently bound to a live port**, with two flows that must exchange their fates.

```
awplus(config)#access-list hardware ckdyn
% This access-list is attached to an interface. The modified configuration will be applied to the interface automatically after exiting this configuration mode  by entering "exit" or "end" command.
awplus(config-ip-hw-acl)#no deny tcp any any eq 22
awplus(config-ip-hw-acl)#deny icmp any any
awplus(config-ip-hw-acl)#end                         [box 14:16:45 NZDT]
```
🔴 That `% ` line is an **ADVISORY, not a rejection** -- `platforms/x960.md` section 5 records
the pair of near-identical messages whose difference is one word (`is attached` = advisory,
`is currently attached` = rejection). `cfg.py` carried on correctly because the string is in
`console.ADVISORY`. **Had it been treated as an error, this step would have read as a product
failure when the product was working.** The same note also tells you *when* the change lands:
not at the rule, but on leaving the sub-mode.

Read back 7 s later, at box 14:16:52:
```
awplus#show access-list
Hardware IP access list ckdyn
    4 deny icmp any any
```

```
sudo arpleg.py --tag dyn-change ... --iface eth3 --tcp 10.38.22.34:22 --icmp 10.38.22.34 ... --count 4
  cap dyn-change-eth3.pcap: 18 frame(s)
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.33    :40000 -> 10.38.22.34    :22    S    [ours]
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.34    :22    -> 10.38.22.33    :40000 SA
    x4     TCP  10.38.22.33    :40000 -> 10.38.22.34    :22    R
    x4     ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.33     -> 10.38.22.34     [ours]
    x1     ARP-REQ  who-has 10.38.22.34     tell 10.38.22.33
    x1     ARP-REPLY 10.38.22.34     is-at e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43
```

| flow | STEP 1 | STEP 2 (added) | STEP 3 (changed) |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| TCP/22, 4 SYNs | 4 `SA` | **0** | **4 `SA`** -- unblocked again |
| ICMP echo, 4 | 4 replies | 4 replies | **0** -- now blocked |

**Both flows changed state on one edit, in opposite directions.** That is not something a
stale dataplane could fake, and it is not something a read-back could have told you.

The device's own counter agrees with the wire:
```
awplus#show access-list counters
Name: ckdyn
	Rule: deny icmp any any
	Packet Hits: 4
```
4 hits = the 4 echo requests.

**STEP 3 verdict: PASS -- change is effected dynamically, including on a filter already bound
to a live port.**

